    'cyber_security-cryptography-s1-set1' => [
        [
            'text' => 'Compare Symmetric Key Cryptography with Asymmetric Key Cryptography (Public Key Cryptography). What are the computational performance and key distribution trade-offs of each?',
            'category' => 'technical',
            'duration_seconds' => 180,
            'note' => 'Symmetric uses single shared secret key for encryption and decryption (ultra-fast, AES, key distribution problem at scale); Asymmetric uses public key to encrypt and private key to decrypt (computationally 1000x slower, RSA/ECC, solves key distribution).',
        ],
        [
            'text' => 'Explain the Advanced Encryption Standard (AES). What is the block size of AES, what key lengths (128, 192, 256 bits) are standard, and how many transformation rounds execute for each?',
            'category' => 'technical',
            'duration_seconds' => 180,
            'note' => 'Symmetric block cipher based on Substitution-Permutation Network; fixed block size of 128 bits (16 bytes); key lengths: 128-bit (10 rounds), 192-bit (12 rounds), 256-bit (14 rounds). Rounds apply SubBytes, ShiftRows, MixColumns, and AddRoundKey.',
        ],
        [
            'text' => 'What are Cryptographic Hash Functions? Define the core properties: One-Way (Pre-image resistance), Second Pre-image resistance, and Collision resistance.',
            'category' => 'technical',
            'duration_seconds' => 180,
            'note' => 'Pre-image resistance: given hash h, computationally infeasible to find input m ($H(m)=h$); Second pre-image resistance: given m1, infeasible to find $m2 \\neq m1$ where $H(m1)=H(m2)$; Collision resistance: infeasible to find ANY arbitrary pair $(m1, m2)$ where $H(m1)=H(m2)$.',
        ],
        [
            'text' => 'Explain RSA public key cryptography mathematics. How do prime factor generation (p, q), modulus ($n = p \\times q$), Euler\'s totient ($\\phi(n)$), and modular inverse derive public key (e) and private key (d)?',
            'category' => 'technical',
            'duration_seconds' => 180,
            'note' => 'Pick large primes p, q; $n = pq$; $\\phi(n) = (p-1)(q-1)$; choose public exponent e coprime to $\\phi(n)$ (typically 65537); compute private key $d \\equiv e^{-1} \\pmod{\\phi(n)}$. Encryption: $c = m^e \\pmod n$; Decryption: $m = c^d \\pmod n$. Relies on prime factorization hardness.',
        ],
        [
            'text' => 'What is the Birthday Paradox in cryptography, and how does it explain why an N-bit hash function provides only N/2 bits of resistance against collision attacks?',
            'category' => 'technical',
            'duration_seconds' => 180,
            'note' => 'In a room of 23 people, there is a 50% chance two share a birthday (comparing all pairs, $O(N^2)$). For an N-bit hash with $2^N$ outputs, evaluating only $\\approx \\sqrt{2^N} = 2^{N/2}$ random inputs yields a 50% probability of finding a hash collision.',
        ],
    ],
    'cyber_security-cryptography-s1-set2' => [
        [
            'text' => 'Compare Block Cipher Modes of Operation: ECB (Electronic Codebook), CBC (Cipher Block Chaining), and GCM (Galois/Counter Mode). Why is ECB mode fundamentally insecure for structured data?',
            'category' => 'technical',
            'duration_seconds' => 180,
            'note' => 'ECB encrypts identical plaintext blocks into identical ciphertext blocks, preserving visual data patterns (Tux penguin); CBC XORs previous ciphertext block with current plaintext (requires IV); GCM turns block cipher into stream cipher with parallel CTR mode and built-in GMAC authentication.',
        ],
        [
            'text' => 'What is a Message Authentication Code (MAC) and HMAC (Hash-Based MAC)? How does HMAC verify both data integrity and data authenticity, and why is simple \'Hash(Key || Message)\' insecure?',
            'category' => 'technical',
            'duration_seconds' => 180,
            'note' => 'HMAC = $H((K \\oplus opad) || H((K \\oplus ipad) || M))$. Verifies message has not been altered and was authored by shared key holder. Naive Hash(Key || Message) is vulnerable to Length Extension Attacks in Merkle-Damgard hashes (MD5, SHA-1, SHA-256).',
