    'cyber_security-cryptography-s2-set1' => [
        [
            'text' => 'Compare Block Ciphers with Stream Ciphers. How does ChaCha20 operate as a stream cipher, and why is ChaCha20-Poly1305 faster than AES on mobile devices lacking AES-NI hardware instructions?',
            'category' => 'technical',
            'duration_seconds' => 180,
            'note' => 'Block ciphers operate on fixed blocks; Stream ciphers generate pseudo-random keystream XORed with plaintext bit-by-bit. ChaCha20 uses ARX (Add-Rotate-Xor) operations that execute in constant time across standard CPU registers without requiring dedicated AES-NI silicon.',
        ],
        [
            'text' => 'What are Initialization Vectors (IV) and Nonces? What cryptographic disaster occurs when the same Nonce is reused with the same key in AES-GCM or ChaCha20?',
            'category' => 'technical',
            'duration_seconds' => 180,
            'note' => 'Nonces provide unique entropy preventing identical plaintexts from producing identical ciphertexts. In stream ciphers / GCM, reusing a Nonce with same key reveals XOR of plaintexts ($C_1 \\oplus C_2 = P_1 \\oplus P_2$) and allows recovering the GMAC authentication key, enabling total forgery.',
